# KORVANE COLD CHAIN Sp. z o.o. — Employee Handbook, Version 4 (Extract)

Document ref. HR-HB-04 / EXTRACT-C · Effective 1 February 2024 · Owner: People & Culture (Ines Baptista-Okafor) · Printed copies are uncontrolled, please check the intranet for the live text.

*This extract reproduces Sections 3, 4, 5, 6, 8 and 12 of the Handbook for display on depot notice boards and for inclusion in welcome packs. Where this extract and the full Handbook differ, the full Handbook shall prevail; where the Handbook and a signed individual contract differ, the contract shall prevail to the extent that it is more favourable to the employee. Nothing herein creates a contractual entitlement beyond those expressly incorporated into an employment contract.*

## Section 3 — Annual Leave

3.1 Annual leave is granted in working days and accrues by reference to completed years of service with the Company. Service with an entity acquired by the Company is counted; periods of unpaid leave exceeding 30 consecutive days are not counted. For a full-time employee the following entitlements apply per leave year:[1]

| Completed years of service | Working days of leave per leave year |
|---|---|
| 0 to 2 | 21 |
| 3 to 5 | 24 |
| 6 to 9 | 27 |
| 10 and over | 30 |

3.2 The leave year is the calendar year. In the year of joining, leave is credited at one twelfth of the annual figure for each full calendar month of employment, rounded up to the nearest half day. Part-time employees are credited pro rata to their contractual hours.

3.3 Requests are to be submitted on form PD-12 (intranet, "People Desk") at least 10 working days before the first day of any absence exceeding 5 consecutive working days; for shorter absences two working days' notice shall suffice. The line manager is expected to respond within 4 working days. Silence is not approval: a request that has not been answered within that period is escalated automatically to the Depot Manager (or, for office staff, the relevant Head of Department).

3.4 Between 10 November and 23 December no more than one in five members of any operational shift team at GDY-1 or RTM-3 may be on annual leave at the same time, save with the written consent of the Depot Manager concerned. The same restriction has, by custom rather than by rule, been applied informally at HAM-2.

3.5 Up to 6 days of unused leave may be carried over into the following leave year and must be taken by 31 May. Days above the carry-over limit shall be scheduled unilaterally by the Company, which will notify the employee of the dates no later than 30 November.

3.6 Special paid leave (not deducted from annual leave) is granted as follows: two days for the employee's own wedding; four days on the death of a spouse, child or parent, and two days on the death of a sibling or grandparent; one day per calendar year for moving house, on production of a rental or purchase document. In addition, 15 May (Founders' Day) is a half day, with work ending at 12:00 for all employees not rostered on a live reefer-yard shift; rostered employees receive the half day back as time in lieu.

[1] Version 3 of the Handbook (1 September 2021) granted 20 days in the entry tier. The v3 figure is quoted here only because a number of older individual contracts still refer to it by number; those employees are entitled to the higher v4 figure from 1 February 2024.

## Section 4 — Sickness Absence and Sick Pay

4.1 An employee who is unable to attend work shall inform the shift lead (operations staff) or the line manager (office staff) by telephone no later than one hour after the scheduled start of the shift; office staff who do not work to a fixed shift must call by 09:30. Notification by text message or through a colleague is not sufficient unless the shift lead has expressly agreed it in advance.

4.2 An electronic medical certificate (e-ZLA) must be available to the Company within 3 working days of the first day of absence. For absences of up to 2 consecutive working days an employee may self-certify, but not more than 4 days of self-certified absence are accepted in any calendar year.

4.3 Sick pay is paid by the Company in steps, counted cumulatively over each period of twelve months starting from the first day of the first absence:

| Step | Days of absence | Level of pay |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Days 1 to 3 | 100% of base pay |
| 2 | Days 4 to 33 | 90% of base pay |
| 3 | Days 34 to 90 | 88% of base pay for employees with at least 3 completed years of service; statutory sickness benefit only for all others |
| 4 | Day 91 onwards | Statutory sickness benefit only |

4.4 A return-to-work conversation is held after any single absence of 10 or more consecutive working days, and after the third separate absence within any rolling period of six months, irrespective of the length of the individual absences. The conversation is conducted by the line manager and recorded on form PD-31; it is not a disciplinary meeting and is not to be treated as one.

## Section 5 — Remote and Hybrid Working

5.1 Remote working is open to roles which do not require physical presence at a depot, once the employee has completed the probation period of 3 months. Operations staff on a shift roster are not eligible. Dispatchers may work from home only when the Head of Operations has declared a contingency, for example an official closure of a depot or a flood warning for the Gdynia yard.

5.2 An eligible employee may work remotely for up to 2 days per week, and in no event for more than 8 days in any calendar month.

5.3 Tuesday and Wednesday are core office days on which all office-based employees attend ul. Łużycka 14 unless on leave or travelling. Core hours are 10:00 to 15:00 on every working day, whether in the office or remote.

5.4 A home-office allowance of PLN 60 gross per month is paid to employees who work remotely on at least 4 days per month on average. A one-off equipment stipend of PLN 450 (monitor or chair, against receipt) is available once in every 36 months.

5.5 Work from abroad is permitted in EU or EEA countries only, for no more than 20 working days per calendar year, and only with written approval from the line manager and People & Culture obtained at least 15 working days before the first day abroad. (Tax and social-security consequences are for the Company to assess, not the employee; please do not book flights first.)

## Section 6 — Business Travel and Expenses

6.1 Expenses are claimed on form EX-7 within 30 calendar days of the end of the trip. Claims submitted later require the approval of the Finance Controller. Approved claims are paid together with the payroll on the 10th of the month following the month of approval.

6.2 The following ceilings apply to receipted costs per person (they are ceilings, not allowances; no one is paid for what has not been spent):

| Country | Meals per day | Hotel per night, breakfast included | Private car per km |
|---|---|---|---|
| Poland | PLN 118 | PLN 430 | PLN 1.27 |
| Germany | EUR 36 | EUR 128 | EUR 0.34[2] |
| Netherlands | EUR 39 | EUR 146 | EUR 0.36 |
| Sweden | EUR 41 (paid in SEK at the ECB rate on the day of travel) | EUR 152 | EUR 0.33 |
| Lithuania | EUR 29 | EUR 97 | EUR 0.27 |

6.3 A receipt is required for every single item above PLN 65 (EUR 15). Alcohol is not reimbursed under any circumstances, including at client dinners.

6.4 Flights shall be booked in economy class for journeys of under 6 hours. Rail travel is second class. Taxis may be used between 22:00 and 05:00, or where the traveller carries luggage above 20 kg; otherwise public transport is expected.

6.5 A trip with an estimated cost above EUR 500 requires prior approval of the Head of Department; a trip estimated above EUR 2,500 additionally requires the signature of the CFO, Priya Venkataraman-Holt. Client entertainment is limited to EUR 70 per head and must be approved in advance by the CFO's office.

[2] Under v3 the German mileage rate was EUR 0.29 per km. Do not use it. Several expense claims for Q1 2024 still arrived at the old rate and had to be recalculated by hand.

## Section 8 — Disciplinary Procedure

8.1 Disciplinary action proceeds up a ladder. A stage may be skipped only with the approval of People & Culture.

| Stage | Sanction | Stays on the personnel file for |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Documented informal conversation | 6 months |
| 2 | Written warning | 12 months |
| 3 | Final written warning | 18 months |
| 4 | Termination of employment, with notice (or without notice in the cases listed in 8.4) | — |

8.2 A sanction may be imposed only within 10 working days after the line manager became aware of the conduct complained of. The employee is invited to a disciplinary meeting in writing at least 3 working days beforehand and may be accompanied by a colleague or a trade-union representative. The decision is delivered in writing within 5 working days after the meeting.

8.3 An appeal may be lodged with the Head of People & Culture within 7 calendar days of receipt of the decision. It is heard by a panel of two, one of whom comes from the Legal department, and is decided within 10 working days. The sanction is not suspended pending appeal.

8.4 The following are gross misconduct and may lead to summary dismissal after a hearing: falsification of a temperature or door-open record (code GM-1); reporting for a shift with a breath-alcohol reading above 0.10 per mille (GM-2); theft (GM-3); disclosure of customer data to a third party without authorisation (GM-4); deliberate disabling of a KC-Tag 4 or KC-Hub 200 alarm (GM-5); violence or threats of violence (GM-6). Pending investigation, an employee may be suspended on full pay for up to 15 working days.

## Section 12 — Termination and Notice

12.1 During the probation period of 3 months, either party may terminate with 5 working days' notice.

12.2 After probation, the notice to be given by the Company is: two weeks if the employee has been employed for less than 6 months in total; one month if employed for 6 months up to 3 years; and 3 months if employed for more than 3 years.[3] The employee gives one month's notice in grades O1 to O4 and two months' notice in grade O5 and above, and in every managerial role.

12.3 The Company may release the employee from the duty to work during notice ("garden leave"), in which case base pay continues in full.

12.4 All Company property (badge, PPE, laptop, handheld tag reader) must be returned within 3 working days after the last day of employment. Final pay is settled by the 10th of the following month; accrued unused leave is paid out at one twenty-first and a half (1/21.5) of monthly base pay per day.

12.5 A certificate of employment is handed over on the last working day. An exit interview is offered during the last 5 working days.

[3] Under v3 the Company's notice for employees with more than 3 years of service was 2 months.

*End of extract. Full text: Handbook v4, 16 sections plus Annexes A to D (intranet > People Desk > Policies). HR-HB-04/EXTRACT-C, rev. C — uncontrolled when printed.*
